import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
---
<PageLayout
  title="Mission"
  eyebrow="Our mission"
  heading="No thinker should have to build alone."
  intro="Thinker's Journal is a non-profit that helps thinkers of every kind bring what's in their heads into the world — free, at cost, or below market.">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Every day, someone has an idea worth building — and no way to build it alone. We exist for them: the programmers, the makers, the quietly relentless people who can't stop turning a better version of something over in their minds.</p>
    <p>We give them what they've been missing — a mentor who's been there, a partner who <em>gets it</em>, the tools to build, and a place to be taken seriously — offered freely, or as close to free as we can make it. Because a good idea should never die for lack of help.</p>
    <p>We started with programmers, because that's where our roots run forty-four years deep. But the mission is bigger than any one craft: <strong>no thinker should have to build alone.</strong></p>
    <h2>Why I'm building this</h2>
    <p>I didn't get here alone. In more than forty-four years of programming, there was always someone further down the path who answered the question I was embarrassed to ask, looked over work I wasn't sure about, or just let me vent until I could think straight again. I'm still at it because of them.</p>
    <p>Thinker's Journal is me paying that forward.</p>
    <p>Building something big is daunting when you're starting out, and what carries people through it is rarely brilliance — it's having someone there. Someone to answer the question, check the work, or listen. That's what kept me going, and I've never forgotten it.</p>
    <p>The people who need that most are often the least able to buy it. Some are simply broke. Others could spare a little but won't spend it on themselves yet, because they don't believe they're good enough to be worth it. That's backwards — and it's exactly why we don't charge them.</p>
    <p>So we give them whatever they need: <strong>an ear, a server, or a crutch to lean on until they believe in themselves.</strong></p>
    <p class="sign">— the founder</p>
    <h2>Why non-profit</h2>
    <p>There are no investors to repay here and no margin to protect. That's what lets us keep help free, at cost, or below market — and keep it that way when it would be more convenient not to. Thinker's Journal is a non-profit in formation, funded by people who believe in the mission, run as leanly and transparently as we can manage.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
